   "id": "993ceded",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# One-dimensional Bayes localisation\n",
    "\n",
    "A discrete Bayes (histogram) filter implemented with NumPy, with simulation experiments on localisation accuracy and calibration. The method follows Thrun, Burgard and Fox (2005), *Probabilistic Robotics*, chapter 4.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Measurement update on three cells, checked against a worked calculation\n",
    "2. Measurement and motion updates on a loop of 10 cells\n",
    "3. Range-sensor localisation in a 20 m corridor\n",
    "4. Calibration of the 90% credible region\n",
    "5. Outlier readings\n",
    "6. Limitations"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "622d521c",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-11-25T11:37:55.265288Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-11-25T11:37:55.265119Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-11-25T11:37:55.811110Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-11-25T11:37:55.809914Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "\n",
    "Path(\"figures\").mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n",
    "plt.rcParams.update({\"figure.dpi\": 110, \"axes.spines.top\": False, \"axes.spines.right\": False})\n",
    "\n",
    "def entropy(p):\n",
    "    # Shannon entropy in nats; the maximum for N cells is ln(N).\n",
    "    p = p[p > 0]\n",
    "    return float(-(p * np.log(p)).sum())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dc798ad8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Measurement update\n",
    "\n",
    "A robot occupies one of three cells (wall, door, wall) with a uniform prior of 1/3. The door sensor is correct with probability 0.8. After a \"door\" reading, Bayes' rule gives P(cell | reading) proportional to P(reading | cell) P(cell).\n",
    "\n",
    "The likelihoods are 0.2, 0.8 and 0.2, so the unnormalised posterior is (0.2, 0.8, 0.2)/3, which sums to 1.2/3. The normalised posterior is (1/6, 2/3, 1/6). The code below reproduces this result."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "7675984c",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-11-25T11:37:55.813875Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-11-25T11:37:55.813614Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-11-25T11:37:55.821850Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-11-25T11:37:55.820785Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "after one 'door' reading : [0.1667 0.6667 0.1667]\n",
      "after a second reading   : [0.0556 0.8889 0.0556]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "world3 = np.array([\"wall\", \"door\", \"wall\"])\n",
    "belief3 = np.full(3, 1 / 3)\n",
    "\n",
    "def sense_discrete(belief, world, reading, p_correct):\n",
    "    # Likelihood of the reading in each cell: p_correct if the cell matches, else 1 - p_correct.\n",
    "    likelihood = np.where(world == reading, p_correct, 1 - p_correct)\n",
    "    posterior = likelihood * belief          # Bayes' rule, unnormalised\n",
    "    return posterior / posterior.sum()       # normalise so the belief sums to 1\n",
    "\n",
    "after_one = sense_discrete(belief3, world3, \"door\", 0.8)\n",
    "print(\"after one 'door' reading :\", np.round(after_one, 4))\n",
    "assert np.allclose(after_one, [1 / 6, 2 / 3, 1 / 6])      # matches the worked calculation\n",
    "\n",
    "after_two = sense_discrete(after_one, world3, \"door\", 0.8)\n",
    "print(\"after a second reading   :\", np.round(after_two, 4))\n",
    "# Worked calculation: [0.2/6, 0.8*2/3, 0.2/6] = [0.0333, 0.5333, 0.0333]; they sum to 0.6, so divide: [0.0556, 0.8889, 0.0556]\n",
    "assert np.allclose(after_two, [1 / 18, 8 / 9, 1 / 18])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "511fd1dd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Two consistent readings raise the belief in the middle cell from 1/3 to 2/3 to 8/9."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "77f58877",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Measurement and motion updates on a loop\n",
    "\n",
    "The world is a loop of 10 cells containing doors (D) and walls (W). A command to move one cell succeeds with probability 0.8, leaves the robot in place with probability 0.1 and moves it two cells with probability 0.1. Under the Markov assumption, the motion update is a weighted sum of shifted copies of the belief. The filter alternates a measurement update and a motion update."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "3119f3b4",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-11-25T11:37:55.823660Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-11-25T11:37:55.823486Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-11-25T11:37:56.830663Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-11-25T11:37:56.829631Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "one certain cell after a move: [0.  0.  0.  0.1 0.8 0.1 0.  0.  0.  0. ]\n",
      "true cells: [3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 0, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 0, 1, 3, 4, 5]\n",
      "readings  : ['W', 'W', 'W', 'D', 'W', 'W', 'W', 'W', 'W', 'W', 'D', 'D', 'D', 'W', 'W', 'W', 'D', 'W', 'D', 'W']\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1210x506 with 8 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "maximum entropy (uniform belief): 2.30\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "best guess equals the true cell at steps 0, 3, 6, 10, 19: [0.12 0.34 0.34 0.37 0.35] (chance = 0.10)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "world10 = np.array(list(\"WDWWDWDWWW\"))       # W = wall, D = door; doors at cells 1, 4 and 6\n",
    "N10 = len(world10)\n",
    "\n",
    "def move_discrete(belief, p_exact=0.8, p_under=0.1, p_over=0.1, step=1):\n",
    "    # np.roll shifts the belief by a number of cells (wrapping around the loop)\n",
    "    return (p_exact * np.roll(belief, step)\n",
    "            + p_under * np.roll(belief, step - 1)\n",
    "            + p_over * np.roll(belief, step + 1))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Sanity checks: a move conserves probability, and blurs a certain belief into three cells\n",
    "spike = np.zeros(N10); spike[3] = 1\n",
    "moved = move_discrete(spike)\n",
    "assert np.isclose(moved.sum(), 1)\n",
    "print(\"one certain cell after a move:\", np.round(moved, 2))\n",
    "\n",
    "def simulate_discrete(rng, T=20, start=3, p_sense=0.8):\n",
    "    # Generate what the robot actually does and reads. The filter below only sees the readings.\n",
    "    pos, truth, readings = start, [], []\n",
    "    for _ in range(T):\n",
    "        correct = str(world10[pos])\n",
    "        wrong = \"W\" if correct == \"D\" else \"D\"\n",
    "        readings.append(correct if rng.random() < p_sense else wrong)\n",
    "        truth.append(int(pos))\n",
    "        pos = int((pos + rng.choice([1, 0, 2], p=[0.8, 0.1, 0.1])) % N10)\n",
    "    return truth, readings\n",
    "\n",
    "rng = np.random.default_rng(3)\n",
    "truth10, readings10 = simulate_discrete(rng)\n",
    "print(\"true cells:\", truth10)\n",
    "print(\"readings  :\", readings10)\n",
    "\n",
    "belief = np.full(N10, 1 / N10)\n",
    "beliefs10 = []\n",
    "for z in readings10:\n",
    "    belief = sense_discrete(belief, world10, z, 0.8)\n",
    "    beliefs10.append(belief.copy())\n",
    "    belief = move_discrete(belief)\n",
    "\n",
    "show = [0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 12, 19]                    # steps to draw\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 4, figsize=(11, 4.6), sharey=True)\n",
    "for t, ax in zip(show, axes.ravel()):\n",
    "    b = beliefs10[t]\n",
    "    colours = [\"#c0504d\" if world10[i] == \"D\" else \"#9aa5b1\" for i in range(N10)]\n",
    "    ax.bar(range(N10), b, color=colours)\n",
    "    ax.axvline(truth10[t], color=\"black\", lw=1.2, ls=\"--\")\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f\"step {t}: read '{readings10[t]}'   H={entropy(b):.2f}\", fontsize=9)\n",
    "    ax.set_xticks(range(N10))\n",
    "fig.supxlabel(\"cell (red = door, grey = wall, dashed line = true position)\")\n",
    "fig.supylabel(\"belief\")\n",
    "fig.suptitle(\"Belief after the measurement update at selected steps (H = entropy)\", fontsize=11)\n",
    "fig.tight_layout()\n",
    "fig.savefig(\"figures/discrete_filter.png\", dpi=130)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "print(\"maximum entropy (uniform belief): %.2f\" % np.log(N10))\n",
    "\n",
    "# How often is the single most probable cell the true cell? 300 runs, random starting cells.\n",
    "hits = np.zeros(20)\n",
    "for s in range(300):\n",
    "    r = np.random.default_rng(1000 + s)\n",
    "    tr, rd = simulate_discrete(r, T=20, start=int(r.integers(N10)))\n",
    "    b = np.full(N10, 1 / N10)\n",
    "    for t, z in enumerate(rd):\n",
    "        b = sense_discrete(b, world10, z, 0.8)\n",
    "        hits[t] += (b.argmax() == tr[t])\n",
    "        b = move_discrete(b)\n",
    "hits /= 300\n",
    "print(\"best guess equals the true cell at steps 0, 3, 6, 10, 19:\", np.round(hits[[0, 3, 6, 10, 19]], 2), \"(chance = 0.10)\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "04769447",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The belief concentrates around door cells but does not converge to a single cell. Motion noise adds uncertainty at each step, and a door/wall reading is weakly informative because seven of the ten cells are walls. Over 300 runs from random starting cells, the most probable cell was the true cell about one third of the time (35% at step 19; chance level 10%)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7278dcf5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Range-sensor localisation in a corridor\n",
    "\n",
    "The corridor is 20 m long, discretised into 201 cells of 0.1 m, with five landmarks at known positions. The sensor returns the signed distance to the nearest landmark with Gaussian noise (σ = 0.25 m) and does not identify which landmark was observed. Each command moves the robot 0.5 m with Gaussian error (σ = 0.15 m), and the robot stops at the corridor ends. The prior is uniform.\n",
    "\n",
    "The initial belief is multimodal. Motion resolves the ambiguity because the landmark spacings differ."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "d56d328b",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-11-25T11:37:56.832884Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-11-25T11:37:56.832585Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-11-25T11:37:57.314317Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-11-25T11:37:57.313274Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1320x462 with 4 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "entropy after the first reading: 3.86 nats (uniform belief: 5.30)\n",
      "first step with >90% of belief within 0.5 m of the best guess: step 8\n",
      "error at the last step: 0.16 m (true 15.66 m, estimate 15.50 m)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "L, dx = 20.0, 0.1\n",
    "xs = np.arange(0, L + dx / 2, dx)                  # cell centres, 201 of them\n",
    "N = len(xs)\n",
    "landmarks = np.array([2.0, 5.5, 6.5, 13.0, 17.0])  # known map (metres)\n",
    "SIGMA_S_TRUE, SIGMA_M_TRUE, STEP = 0.25, 0.15, 0.5\n",
    "\n",
    "def range_to_nearest(x):\n",
    "    # Signed distance from position(s) x to the nearest landmark\n",
    "    x = np.atleast_1d(x)\n",
    "    d = landmarks[None, :] - x[:, None]\n",
    "    nearest = np.abs(d).argmin(axis=1)\n",
    "    return d[np.arange(len(x)), nearest]\n",
    "\n",
    "expected_z = range_to_nearest(xs)    # what a perfect sensor would read in every cell\n",
    "\n",
    "def gaussian(z, mu, sigma):\n",
    "    return np.exp(-0.5 * ((z - mu) / sigma) ** 2) / (sigma * np.sqrt(2 * np.pi))\n",
    "\n",
    "def sense_range(belief, z, sigma_s):\n",
    "    # Bayes' rule: Gaussian likelihood of the reading in each cell, times the prior belief\n",
    "    posterior = gaussian(z, expected_z, sigma_s) * belief\n",
    "    total = posterior.sum()\n",
    "    assert total > 0, \"reading is impossible under this model\"\n",
    "    return posterior / total\n",
    "\n",
    "def move_range(belief, u, sigma_m):\n",
    "    # Spread each cell's belief over the cells it might end up in, using a Gaussian on the move\n",
    "    half = int(np.ceil(5 * sigma_m / dx))\n",
    "    centre = int(round(u / dx))\n",
    "    ks = np.arange(centre - half, centre + half + 1)        # possible moves, in cells\n",
    "    w = gaussian(ks * dx, u, sigma_m)\n",
    "    w /= w.sum()\n",
    "    new = np.zeros_like(belief)\n",
    "    idx = np.arange(N)\n",
    "    for k, wk in zip(ks, w):\n",
    "        np.add.at(new, np.clip(idx + k, 0, N - 1), belief * wk)   # clip: the robot cannot leave the corridor\n",
    "    return new\n",
    "\n",
    "def simulate_run(rng, T=30):\n",
    "    # The robot's true path and the noisy readings it would collect along it\n",
    "    x = rng.uniform(0.0, 4.0)\n",
    "    xs_true, zs = [], []\n",
    "    for _ in range(T):\n",
    "        xs_true.append(x)\n",
    "        zs.append(range_to_nearest(x)[0] + rng.normal(0, SIGMA_S_TRUE))\n",
    "        x = float(np.clip(x + STEP + rng.normal(0, SIGMA_M_TRUE), 0, L))\n",
    "    return np.array(xs_true), np.array(zs)\n",
    "\n",
    "def run_filter(zs, sigma_s, sigma_m):\n",
    "    # Measurement update, then motion update, at every step. Returns the belief after each measurement update.\n",
    "    belief = np.full(N, 1 / N)\n",
    "    out = []\n",
    "    for z in zs:\n",
    "        belief = sense_range(belief, z, sigma_s)\n",
    "        out.append(belief.copy())\n",
    "        belief = move_range(belief, STEP, sigma_m)\n",
    "    return np.array(out)\n",
    "\n",
    "# One demonstration run\n",
    "rng = np.random.default_rng(7)\n",
    "x_true, zs = simulate_run(rng)\n",
    "B = run_filter(zs, SIGMA_S_TRUE, SIGMA_M_TRUE)\n",
    "\n",
    "map_est = xs[B.argmax(axis=1)]                      # most probable position at each step\n",
    "err = map_est - x_true\n",
    "H = np.array([entropy(b) for b in B])\n",
    "mass_near = np.array([b[np.abs(xs - m) <= 0.5].sum() for b, m in zip(B, map_est)])\n",
    "first_confident = int(np.argmax(mass_near > 0.9))   # first step with >90% belief within 0.5 m of the best guess\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 4.2), gridspec_kw={\"width_ratios\": [1.6, 1]})\n",
    "im = ax1.imshow(B.T, origin=\"lower\", aspect=\"auto\", extent=[-0.5, len(zs) - 0.5, 0, L], cmap=\"viridis\")\n",
    "ax1.plot(range(len(zs)), x_true, \"w--\", lw=1.4, label=\"true position\")\n",
    "for lm in landmarks:\n",
    "    ax1.axhline(lm, color=\"#e06666\", lw=0.6, alpha=0.8)\n",
    "ax1.set_xlabel(\"step\"); ax1.set_ylabel(\"position (m)\")\n",
    "ax1.set_title(\"Belief over position (red lines = landmarks)\")\n",
    "ax1.legend(loc=\"lower right\", fontsize=8)\n",
    "fig.colorbar(im, ax=ax1, label=\"belief\")\n",
    "ax2.plot(H, color=\"#4472c4\", label=\"entropy of belief\")\n",
    "ax2.set_xlabel(\"step\"); ax2.set_ylabel(\"entropy (nats)\", color=\"#4472c4\")\n",
    "ax2b = ax2.twinx()\n",
    "ax2b.plot(np.abs(err), color=\"#c0504d\", label=\"|best guess - truth|\")\n",
    "ax2b.set_ylabel(\"error (m)\", color=\"#c0504d\")\n",
    "ax2b.spines[\"right\"].set_visible(True)\n",
    "ax2.set_title(\"Uncertainty and error over time\")\n",
    "fig.tight_layout()\n",
    "fig.savefig(\"figures/range_filter.png\", dpi=130)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"entropy after the first reading: %.2f nats (uniform belief: %.2f)\" % (H[0], np.log(N)))\n",
    "print(\"first step with >90%% of belief within 0.5 m of the best guess: step %d\" % first_confident)\n",
    "print(\"error at the last step: %.2f m (true %.2f m, estimate %.2f m)\" % (abs(err[-1]), x_true[-1], map_est[-1]))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "60a00405",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Calibration\n",
    "\n",
    "A 90% credible region should contain the true position in about 90% of runs. The filter was run on 300 simulated runs generated with the noise levels above. At the final step, the credible region was the smallest set of cells holding 90% of the belief, and coverage was the fraction of runs whose true cell lay inside it. The same data were then filtered with incorrect noise parameters, first too small and then too large."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "de1c29ae",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-11-25T11:37:57.316037Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-11-25T11:37:57.315835Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-11-25T11:38:02.330324Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-11-25T11:38:02.328767Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "overconfident (sensor 0.10, move 0.05)     coverage 0.533 +/- 0.029   mean 90% region width 0.24 m\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "matched to truth (sensor 0.25, move 0.15)  coverage 0.917 +/- 0.016   mean 90% region width 0.57 m\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
