    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "def credible_set(belief, level=0.9):\n",
    "    # Smallest set of cells holding at least `level` of the belief, taking the most probable cells first\n",
    "    order = np.argsort(belief)[::-1]\n",
    "    k = int(np.searchsorted(np.cumsum(belief[order]), level)) + 1\n",
    "    return order[:k]\n",
    "\n",
    "n_runs = 300\n",
    "rng = np.random.default_rng(11)\n",
    "runs = [simulate_run(rng) for _ in range(n_runs)]    # the same data is used for every setting below\n",
    "\n",
    "settings = {\n",
    "    \"overconfident\\n(sensor 0.10, move 0.05)\": (0.10, 0.05),\n",
    "    \"matched to truth\\n(sensor 0.25, move 0.15)\": (SIGMA_S_TRUE, SIGMA_M_TRUE),\n",
    "    \"underconfident\\n(sensor 0.60, move 0.40)\": (0.60, 0.40),\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "results = {}\n",
    "for name, (ss, sm) in settings.items():\n",
    "    hits, widths = [], []\n",
    "    for x_true_run, zs_run in runs:\n",
    "        b_final = run_filter(zs_run, ss, sm)[-1]\n",
    "        cs = credible_set(b_final, 0.9)\n",
    "        true_cell = int(round(x_true_run[-1] / dx))\n",
    "        hits.append(true_cell in cs)\n",
    "        widths.append(len(cs) * dx)\n",
    "    p = float(np.mean(hits))\n",
    "    results[name] = (p, float(np.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n_runs)), float(np.mean(widths)))\n",
    "    print(\"%-42s coverage %.3f +/- %.3f   mean 90%% region width %.2f m\"\n",
    "          % (name.replace(\"\\n\", \" \"), *results[name]))\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7.5, 4))\n",
    "names = list(results)\n",
    "cov = [results[n][0] for n in names]\n",
    "se = [results[n][1] for n in names]\n",
    "ax.bar(range(3), cov, yerr=se, color=[\"#c0504d\", \"#4f9d69\", \"#9aa5b1\"], capsize=4)\n",
    "ax.axhline(0.9, color=\"black\", ls=\"--\", lw=1)\n",
    "ax.text(2.45, 0.91, \"target 0.90\", ha=\"right\", fontsize=9)\n",
    "ax.set_xticks(range(3)); ax.set_xticklabels(names, fontsize=8)\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"coverage of the 90% region\")\n",
    "ax.set_ylim(0, 1.05)\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Calibration over %d simulated runs\" % n_runs)\n",
    "fig.tight_layout()\n",
    "fig.savefig(\"figures/calibration.png\", dpi=130)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4a6fa00e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Coverage was 92% (standard error 1.6 percentage points) when the filter used the true noise levels. With noise parameters that were too small, coverage fell to 53% and the mean region width was 0.24 m, so the region excluded the true position in nearly half of the runs. With noise parameters that were too large, coverage was 99% and the mean width was 1.25 m, compared with 0.57 m for the matched filter."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "62f1da69",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Outlier readings\n",
    "\n",
    "The calibration test was repeated with a fraction of the range readings replaced by a uniform random value between -3 m and 3 m, while the filter still assumed Gaussian noise. Each row uses 300 simulated runs, and the coverage is again measured at the final step. A reading that is impossible under the model counts as a miss.\n",
    "\n",
    "With 10% outliers, coverage of the 90% region fell from 93% to 78% for the filter with the true noise levels, and to 69% at 20% outliers. Widening the assumed sensor noise to 0.60 m recovered part of the coverage, at the cost of larger regions (section 4). The outlier model is simplistic and is not a model of any real sensor."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "06feefa6",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-11-25T11:38:02.332745Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-11-25T11:38:02.332522Z",
