   "source": [
    "def simulate_run_outliers(rng, p_out, T=30):\n",
    "    # Same as simulate_run, except that a fraction p_out of readings is replaced by a random value\n",
    "    x = rng.uniform(0.0, 4.0); xs_true, zs = [], []\n",
    "    for _ in range(T):\n",
    "        xs_true.append(x)\n",
    "        z = range_to_nearest(x)[0] + rng.normal(0, SIGMA_S_TRUE)\n",
    "        if rng.random() < p_out:\n",
    "            z = rng.uniform(-3, 3)          # outlier: random reading between -3 m and 3 m\n",
    "        zs.append(z)\n",
    "        x = float(np.clip(x + STEP + rng.normal(0, SIGMA_M_TRUE), 0, L))\n",
    "    return np.array(xs_true), np.array(zs)\n",
    "\n",
    "def coverage(runs, ss, sm):\n",
    "    # Fraction of runs whose true final cell lies inside the 90% credible region\n",
    "    hits = []\n",
    "    for xt, zs in runs:\n",
    "        try:\n",
    "            b = run_filter(zs, ss, sm)[-1]\n",
    "        except AssertionError:             # reading impossible under the model: count as a miss\n",
    "            hits.append(False); continue\n",
    "        hits.append(int(round(xt[-1] / dx)) in credible_set(b))\n",
    "    return float(np.mean(hits))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"outlier rate | coverage, sensor 0.25 | coverage, sensor 0.60\")\n",
    "for p_out in [0.0, 0.05, 0.10, 0.20]:\n",
    "    rng = np.random.default_rng(11)\n",
    "    runs = [simulate_run_outliers(rng, p_out) for _ in range(300)]\n",
    "    print(\"%12.2f | %21.3f | %21.3f\" % (p_out, coverage(runs, 0.25, 0.15), coverage(runs, 0.60, 0.15)))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "058834b8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "- The simulated noise matches the filter's assumed model, so the matched-noise result is a best case. Outliers were tested in one simple form (section 5); other non-Gaussian errors, map errors and kidnapped-robot events were not tested.\n",
    "- The map and the motion commands are known. The method performs localisation only.\n",
    "- The number of cells grows exponentially with the state dimension. A 20 m by 20 m area at 0.1 m resolution requires 40,000 cells, and adding heading increases this further. Particle filters and Kalman filters are used for larger problems.\n",
    "- Calibration was evaluated at the final step only, over 300 runs.\n",
    "\n",
    "Possible extensions are a particle filter evaluated with the same experiments, and evaluation on real range logs."
   ]
  }
 ],
